        "problem": "From **top to bottom,** the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola $9{y}^{2}-4{x}^{2}=36$ are\n\n$($\n{{field-1}}, \n{{field-2}} $)\\,$\nand\n$\\,($\n{{field-3}}, \n{{field-4}} $).$",
        "worked_solution": "To find the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{y}^{2}-4{x}^{2} & =36 \\\\ 9{y}^{2}-4(0)^{2} & =36 \\\\ 9{y}^{2} & =36 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ y & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, from top to bottom, the $y$-intercepts are $(0,2)$ and $(0,-2).$",
